-- title:
--   Relative degree ψ(N) of k(̄ j,̄ j_N) over k(̄ j)
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, let $N$ be a nonzero natural number, and let $\ell$ be a prime such that $k$ has characteristic $\ell$ and $\ell \nmid N$. Inside the field $k((q))$ of Laurent series over $k$, let $\bar j =$ `jqModC k` denote the Laurent series $q^{-1}$ times the image in $k[[q]]$ of the integral power series `jNum` $= E_4^3\cdot\eta^{-24}$ (coefficientwise reduction along $\mathbb{Z}\to k$), i.e. the reduction of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant, and let $\bar j_N =$ `jqNModC k N` be its image under the substitution $q \mapsto q^N$. The intermediate field `modularFunctionFieldC k N` is the $k$-subfield of $k((q))$ generated by $\{\bar j, \bar j_N\}$. The assertion is that the relative finrank of this field over the $k$-subfield generated by $\bar j$ alone equals `dedekindPsi N`, defined as $\sum_{d \mid N,\ d\ \text{squarefree}} N/d$, the Dedekind $\psi$-function $\psi(N) = N\prod_{p \mid N}(1 + 1/p)$; that is, $[\,k(\bar j,\bar j_N) : k(\bar j)\,] = \psi(N)$.
--
--   This is the degree statement underlying Igusa's good-reduction model of $X_0(N)$ in characteristic $\ell$ for $\ell \nmid N$: reduction of the $q$-expansions preserves the classical degree $[\mathbb{Q}(j,j_N):\mathbb{Q}(j)] = \psi(N)$. It is used throughout the subsequent development of the characteristic-$\ell$ model of $X_0(N)$, its fibres, cusps and Hecke divisors.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_relfinrank_adjoin_jqModC_modularFunctionFieldC_eq_dedekindPsi.lean

